.docname {The interface}
.include {docs}

The window has four parts: the toolbar at the top, the pattern palette on the left, the board canvas in the middle, and the status bar at the bottom. All of them are built with native Bevy UI.

## Toolbar

At 1200 pixels wide and above, the toolbar fits in a single 64-pixel row,
giving the canvas 48 more pixels of height. Subtle dividers separate three groups:

- **Playback:** Play / Pause, Step, the speed slider, Max, and the rate readout (see [Playback and rules](simulation.qd#speed))
- **Board edits:** Randomize, Clear, Undo, and Redo; history buttons are disabled when unavailable
- **Workspace:** Grid, Palette, Import / Export, Settings, and Help

In narrower windows, workspace controls sit on the first row, with playback and
board edits on the second. Every control remains available, including at the
minimum window size. Tab order follows the visible groups, and resizing retains
the focused control, camera pan, and zoom relative to the fitted board.

Hover over or focus a button to see its shortcut.

## Pattern palette

The palette holds the editing tools and the pattern library, from top to bottom:

- the **Draw**, **Erase**, and **Stamp** tools
- a **stamp preview** of the selected pattern in its current orientation, with **Rotate** and **Flip** buttons, shown while a stamp is active
- a **search field** that filters patterns by name, ignoring case
- pattern **thumbnails**, with built-in and imported patterns in separate groups

Only the thumbnails scroll, with the mouse wheel or the scrollbar; the tools, stamp preview, and search stay in place. Filtering the library keeps the current stamp selected, even if the filter hides it, and scrolls back to the first match.

Press .keybinding {H} or click **Palette** to hide or show the palette. Hiding it gives the board the full width of the window. See [Drawing and stamps](editing.qd) for how the tools work.

## Board canvas

The board has its own canvas beside the palette and between the toolbar and status bar. Drawing and stamp previews stay inside it, including on Retina displays.

| Action                         | How                                                       |
|--------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|
| Zoom                           | Scroll over the canvas, or use **− / +** in the status bar |
| Pan                            | Middle-button drag, or .keybinding {W} .keybinding {A} .keybinding {S} .keybinding {D} or the arrow keys |
| Fit the whole board            | .keybinding {V} or **Fit board** in the status bar        |
| Fit the occupied area          | **Fit live cells** in the status bar                     |

The board is fitted into the canvas on startup, after a board size change, and when you press .keybinding {V} or click **Fit board**. When the window size changes or the palette is shown or hidden, the view keeps its pan position and its zoom relative to the fitted view.

The zoom percentage is relative to **Fit board** (100%). **Fit live cells** adds a small margin around the occupied area and falls back to the whole board when empty. Middle-button panning must start on the canvas and stops when the pointer enters a control or leaves the window.

An empty board shows a short hint, which disappears once you place the first cells.

With **fixed** edges, a pale wall outlines the board and pulses briefly when you switch to that mode. With **wrapping** edges, the wall is hidden.

### Grid

Press .keybinding {T} or click **Grid** to draw lines between cells. The grid is drawn only where it is visible, and is skipped when cells are only a few pixels wide, so it costs nothing when zoomed out.

## Status bar

The status bar reads, for example:

```text
RUNNING   ·   Gen 1520   ·   842 alive   ·   B3/S23
```

That is: whether playback is running or paused, the generation counter, the number of living cells, and the rule. Generation and population occupy fixed-width fields with tabular digits, so changing numbers do not move nearby controls. In wider windows, the status bar also shows the board size and edge mode. The navigation controls sit alongside the readouts, or on a second row in narrower windows.

Clear, randomize, undo, and redo briefly show feedback above the status bar. Undoable actions include the **U** shortcut. These notices do not intercept drawing or panning.

## Dialogs

| Dialog              | Opens with                               | Contains                                                       |
|---------------------|------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------|
| **Import / Export** | the **Import / Export** button           | RLE import, and copying the board as RLE                       |
| **Settings**        | the **Settings** button                  | board size, rule, theme, grid, edges, diagnostics, generation reset |
| **Help**            | .keybinding {/} or the **Help** button   | a summary and the full list of shortcuts                       |
| **Diagnostics**     | .keybinding {F3} or **Settings**         | live simulation statistics                                     |

Dialogs are modal. While one is open, typing and scrolling inside it do not reach the board, and board shortcuts are disabled. Press .keybinding {Esc} or click **Close** to close a dialog; focus returns to the control that opened it.

### Diagnostics

The diagnostics dialog shows live statistics while playback continues:

- board size, generation, living cells, and rule
- average step time, as a moving average
- tick interval, or *max speed*
- measured rate, in generations per second
- the number of timed steps
- the dense fallback threshold (see [Simulation engine](engine.qd#frontier-and-dense-stepping))
